WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.600
How does quicksort work? Choose

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.560
a pivot, partition around it,

3
00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.640
then recursively sort the two

4
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.800
sides. The partition itself does

5
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.980
not sort either side, so

6
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.720
do try to resist celebrating

7
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:13.220
early.

8
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.400
Use a half-open range: start

9
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.240
is included and end is

10
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.460
excluded. Stop when its size

11
00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.200
is zero or one, and

12
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.580
place the chosen pivot at

13
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:24.720
end minus one.

14
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.440
Here are six keys: four,

15
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.020
two, six, one, five, three.

16
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:32.540
Keep three at the end

17
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.320
as pivot, and start both

18
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.720
the scan and the boundary

19
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.760
at index zero.

20
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.840
Four is not smaller than

21
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.400
three, so leave it where

22
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.300
it is. Two is smaller,

23
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.100
so swap two with four

24
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.960
at the boundary, then advance

25
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:46.560
the boundary.

26
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.740
Six is not smaller than

27
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:50.760
three, so scan past it.

28
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.460
One is smaller, so swap

29
00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:53.580
one with four at the

30
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.520
boundary, then advance the boundary

31
00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:55.840
again.

32
00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:57.940
Five stays on the large

33
00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.380
side, and the scan is

34
00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.300
finished. Swap the pivot with

35
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.200
six at the boundary: three

36
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.840
now has its final index,

37
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:05.620
two.

38
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.180
The left part is two,

39
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.400
one, which still needs sorting.

40
00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.380
Recurse on the left and

41
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.160
right ranges while excluding the

42
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.497
settled pivot, producing one through

43
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:16.400
six.

44
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.100
Before each scan, everything before

45
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.800
the boundary is smaller than

46
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.620
the pivot. Between the boundary

47
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.420
and scan are processed keys

48
00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.480
at least as large; everything

49
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.980
beyond the scan is still

50
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:29.440
unknown.

51
00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.380
Now derive the cost. A

52
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.240
partition scans a range once,

53
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:35.960
so its work is linear.

54
00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.140
Balanced splits give about log

55
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:39.460
base two of n levels,

56
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.440
with at most linear total

57
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:42.620
work on each level.

58
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.680
If every pivot leaves one

59
00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.800
empty side, the work becomes

60
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.920
n minus one, plus n

61
00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.060
minus two, down to one.

62
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.460
That triangular sum is quadratic,

63
00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.160
and sorted input with a

64
00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.800
last pivot can trigger it.

65
00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.960
For distinct keys, uniform random

66
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.560
pivots give expected n log

67
00:02:01.341 --> 00:02:03.480
n time, but quadratic worst-case

68
00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.340
time remains possible. Expected means

69
00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.980
averaged over the random choices;

70
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:09.520
it is not a promise

71
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:10.460
about every run.

72
00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:12.500
Things to take note of.

73
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.620
Ordinary recursion uses logarithmic stack

74
00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:17.260
space when balanced and linear

75
00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:18.500
space in the worst case,

76
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.640
so this is not constant-space

77
00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.060
under our course definition.

78
00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.960
Recursing only on the smaller

79
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.460
side and looping on the

80
00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.380
larger bounds the stack by

81
00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.640
log n. Quicksort is unstable,

82
00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.418
and many duplicate keys favour

83
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.260
three-way partitioning, which skips the

84
00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:36.020
equal region.

85
00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.880
Keep the pivot convention and

86
00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.440
recursive bounds consistent. A middle

87
00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.360
array position is not necessarily

88
00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.260
a median value, and random

89
00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.700
pivots improve expectation without removing

90
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.260
the worst case. There, now

91
00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:52.200
your partition actually means something.
